<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>MLA Essay Scroll</title>
  <style>
    /* Core Container Setup */
    .scroll-container {
      max-width: 650px; /* Expanded layout to support MLA page margins */
      margin: 20px auto;
      font-family: "Times New Roman", Times, serif; /* STRICT MLA FONT */
      perspective: 1000px;
    }

    /* Original Scroll Rollers (Paper Handles) */
    .scroll-roller {
      background: #d7b98d;
      background: linear-gradient(90deg, #bb9665 0%, #e6ceaa 50%, #bb9665 100%);
      height: 25px;
      border-radius: 4px;
      box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.15);
      position: relative;
      z-index: 10;
    }

    .scroll-roller.bottom {
      cursor: pointer;
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      transition: transform 0.2s ease;
    }

    .scroll-roller.bottom:hover {
      filter: brightness(1.05);
    }

    /* Button Text Styling */
    .scroll-btn-text {
      color: #4a2f0f;
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: bold;
      letter-spacing: 1px;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      user-select: none;
    }

    /* ORIGINAL VINTAGE SCROLL TEXTURE AND SHADOWS */
    .scroll-body {
      background-color: #f4ecd8; /* Warm antique tan color */
      background-image: radial-gradient(rgba(0,0,0,0.03) 1px, transparent 0); /* Paper grain texture */
      background-size: 4px 4px;
      border-left: 2px solid #d5c29d;
      border-right: 2px solid #d5c29d;
      box-shadow: inset 0 0 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.05); /* Inner curved depth shadows */
      
      /* Crucial Animation Properties */
      max-height: 0;
      overflow: hidden;
      transition: max-height 1.8s cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1); /* Smooth 4-page unroll speed */
    }

    /* Expanded State */
    .scroll-body.open {
      max-height: 6000px; /* High ceiling to fit 4 double-spaced pages without cutting off */
    }

    /* MLA CORE TEXT FORMATTING */
    .scroll-content {
      padding: 40px 50px; /* Generous padding to mimic 1-inch margins */
      color: #2b1d0c; /* High contrast dark brown/black text for legibility on texture */
      font-size: 16px; /* High legibility standard scale */
      line-height: 2.0; /* STRICT MLA DOUBLE SPACING */
      text-align: left; /* STRICT MLA LEFT ALIGNMENT */
    }

    .mla-header {
      margin-bottom: 20px;
    }

    .mla-header p {
      margin: 0; /* Keeps line height strictly continuous through double spacing */
    }

    .mla-title {
      font-size: 16px; /* Same text size as body per MLA rules */
      font-weight: normal; /* Normal weights for titles */
      text-align: center;
      margin: 20px 0;
    }

    .mla-paragraph {
      margin: 0 0 20px 0;
      text-indent: 36px; /* Simulates standard 0.5-inch paragraph indent */
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="scroll-container">
  <!-- Top Roller -->
  <div class="scroll-roller top"></div>
  
  <!-- The Scroll Body (Contains your essay) -->
  <div class="scroll-body" id="scrollBody">
    <div class="scroll-content">
      
      <!-- MLA Identification Block -->
      <div class="mla-header">
        <p>Yulian Leon</p>
        <p>Professor Phoebe Vandusen</p>
        <p>ENG 101-FY55</p>
        <p>6 October 2026</p>
      </div>
      
      <!-- MLA Title -->
      <h1 class="mla-title">Beyond the Rosary and Cult Fiction: A Personal Journey to Atheism</h1>
      
      <!-- MLA Body Text (6 Paragraphs) -->
      <div class="mla-text">
        <p class="mla-paragraph">“Oh my god! They killed Kenny!” I am sure many people have heard that quote, their thoughts immediately jumping to South Park. Mine does too, but that’s not the first place I heard those words. Boy Meets World, a sitcom that came out in the 90s, so I doubt many from my generation have even watched it, but it’s my personal favorite show of all time. Anyway, there was an episode that parodied Scream (1996), and it was a dream sequence for Shawn Hunter, one of the main characters. After some random guy is killed, that is when the “Oh my god! They killed Kenny!” came from, which was a reference to South Park, which had debuted months before the episode aired. However, that is not the episode I wanna focus on; it’s a great episode but not the one that left an impression on me. No, the one that had a chokehold on me was “Cult Fiction” (Season 4, Episode 21), serving a crucial role in shaping my understanding and definition of my relationship with religion.</p>
        
        <p class="mla-paragraph">	Religion has always been a complicated subject for me. Mexican culture and Catholicism have always been tied together, whether you fully believe in catholicism or not, but you cannot escape it as a Mexican. From an early age, I knew that, to me, God did not exist; the bible was an ordinary book, and Catholicism held no truth, which is no disrespect to anyone who believes in it. But to me personally, no matter what I did, I could not believe in a religion. I deeply wished I had a religion that resonated with me, something or someone I could turn to when times were hard. Everyone around me always talked about God, Allah, Yahweh, and other names for their religious creator. My mom would always pull me to church either every Saturday or Sunday from ages four to thirteen. The smell of incense coming from the thurible, always filling up and burning my nostrils, is one I could never forget, one that will continue to haunt me.</p>
        
        <p class="mla-paragraph">It was a Tuesday, a school night, 1 A.M., I believe. Watching a TV show should have been the last thing on my mind, but I was almost done with the season, so I might as well continue. I propped my tablet up against a pillow so I could lie on my side and watch. Without paying much attention to the title, I began watching what I only knew was the second-to-last episode. Skipping the intro, the episode opens with Shawn being confronted by his teacher and former guardian regarding his grades. The part that struck out to me was when Shawn was asked, “Who are you going to be?” As the episode progressed, it became abundantly clear that Shawn was struggling to find his footing and, as a result, became vulnerable to joining a cult. Throughout the episode, I had to take a couple of breaks; as I adjusted the pillow I was lying on, I heard footsteps in the living room. Must be my mom, I thought to myself. Remaining completely still until I knew it was safe to press play again; I was in the final moments of the episode. Watching Shawn battle to find a sense of belonging and identity was difficult, especially as someone with their own uncertainties about faith, which is why Shawn’s character resonated with me so deeply in this particular episode. The one thing that stayed with me to this day was when Shawn turned to God and said, “Don’t blow me off, God, I have never asked you for anything, and I never wanted to come to you like this.” With that, the episode ended shortly after. Wiping away the couple of tears that fell, I closed my iPad. The final episode could wait for another day.</p>
        
        <p class="mla-paragraph">Turning onto my back, the only thing I could do was stare at the ceiling. I may be called dramatic for crying over a show. And maybe that is true. But in one single episode, it encapsulated everything I had ever felt towards religion, phrasing the words I had long searched for. Religion has never been a subject I could fully understand in relation to who I am as a person. At least that is what I believed to be true. When I was fifteen and first watched the episode, the feelings of seeing a mirror of myself and understanding my own views about religion were only thoughts I could somewhat grasp; it was not until two years later that these feelings became palpable.</p>
        
        <p class="mla-paragraph">At seventeen, sitting in a hospital, I endured the same vulnerability and conflicting emotions as Shawn did in the show, watching a loved one fight for their life. Surrounded by individuals who turned to God for comfort and relief, I hunted for the same solace, and unsurprisingly, I could not find this sense of hope. Sitting in a booth in the hospital, watching patients’ families, nurses, and doctors eat and converse, I came to the realization that in times of crisis, I do not need to adopt the same religious practices as those around me. In that same moment, the memory of “Cult Fiction” crossed my mind, and through that recall, it allowed for a deeper insight: My sense of self does not require my existence to immerse itself in any religion or philosophy; I can simply be. With that understanding, I have found peace in embracing my own values and navigating life, even if it may differ from my community.</p>
        
        <p class="mla-paragraph">Maybe one day I will choose a religion to dedicate myself to, but for now, embracing the absence of religion, also known as atheism, is what aligns best and most authentically with my own personal values. My childhood often left me feeling that it had already determined my religious views before I had any say. However, without my love for sitcoms, specifically Boy Meets World, and its exposure to various real-life situations, I might not have reached these realizations about myself and religion as early as I did. Whenever I rewatch that episode, I am reminded that no one should feel constrained and bound by their upbringing, familial expectations, or societal norms into adhering to a specific belief system or set of values. Defining your own beliefs requires reflecting on what aligns solely with you and finding what gives you a true sense of comfort and meaning; at the end of the day, it is your own business, not anyone else’s.</p>
      </div>
      
    </div>
  </div>
  
  <!-- Bottom Roller / Clickable Handle -->
  <div class="scroll-roller bottom" onclick="toggleScroll()">
    <span class="scroll-btn-text">Click to Unfold Essay</span>
  </div>
</div>

<script>
  function toggleScroll() {
    const scroll = document.getElementById('scrollBody');
    const btnText = document.querySelector('.scroll-btn-text');
    
    scroll.classList.toggle('open');
    
    if (scroll.classList.contains('open')) {
      btnText.textContent = "Click to Close Essay";
    } else {
      btnText.textContent = "Click to Unfold Essay";
    }
  }
</script>

</body>
</html>
